   "text": "This looks like mezlocillin, a penicillin-type antibiotic. I read it in three parts and took the stereochemistry from the wedges and hashes as drawn.\n\n- **Left ring:** a 2-oxoimidazolidine whose ring N carries a methylsulfonyl group, MeSO2 (CS(=O)(=O)N1CCN(C1=O)\u2026). Its other ring N carries a C(=O)NH urea link.\n- **Middle:** a phenylglycine unit, NH\u2013CH(Ph)\u2013C(=O)\u2013NH. The Ph is on a hashed bond, so this carbon has defined stereochemistry.\n- **Right:** a penam core, a \u03b2-lactam fused to a thiazolidine ring. The thiazolidine bears a gem-dimethyl carbon next to S and a CO2H on a wedge. The ring-fusion H is on a wedge and the amide N at the \u03b2-lactam carbon is on a hash.\n\nI did not derive the @/@@ marks atom by atom from the picture. I assumed the natural penicillin configuration and checked that the chirality tags are consistent with ampicillin's SMILES at the side-chain carbon and the three ring stereocentres.\n\nSMILES: CS(=O)(=O)N1CCN(C1=O)C(=O)N[C@H](c1ccccc1)C(=O)N[C@H]1[C@@H]2N(C1=O)[C@H](C(S2)(C)C)C(=O)O"
